  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 05</p>
  <h1>Non-defining relative clauses: <em>identify first, then add a side view</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">A non-defining relative clause adds a separate comment about a person, thing, place, time, group, or whole event that the listener can already identify. The punctuation is part of the meaning. It tells the reader that the inserted information does not select the referent, even though that information may still be important, sensitive, or open to dispute.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">70–80 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">identity · scope · punctuation</span>
    <span class="tag">who · which · whose · whom</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min extension</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare a selected subgroup with an identified whole.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Keep the relative role and punctuation complete.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Connect</b><span>Use formal, quantity, and whole-clause patterns.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Edit</b><span>Prevent ambiguity and unsupported background claims.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can decide from discourse whether a relative clause identifies a referent or adds a separate assertion; punctuate and voice a parenthetical clause; use <span class="ex">who, whom, which, whose, where</span>, and <span class="ex">when</span> without <span class="ex">that</span> or zero relatives; choose conversational preposition stranding or formal preposition-fronting; build quantity patterns such as <span class="ex">three of whom</span> and <span class="ex">four of which</span>; comment on a whole proposition with <span class="ex">which</span>; repair unclear attachment; avoid hiding disputed claims inside background information; and deliver a layered project briefing whose references remain recoverable.</p>

