  <h2>Get to the root: identification comes from discourse</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A defining relative clause, also called a restrictive clause, helps identify the intended referent. A non-defining relative clause, also called a nonrestrictive clause, comments on a referent that is already recoverable. <span class="ex">Extra</span> means extra for identification. It does not mean unimportant, optional for the writer, or automatically true.</p>
  <p>No noun type guarantees one choice. <span class="ex">My brother who lives in Seoul</span> can be defining if the speaker has more than one brother and must identify which one. <span class="ex">My brother, who lives in Seoul,</span> is non-defining when the listener already knows which brother is meant. A proper name is usually identified in context, but namesakes can require a defining phrase: <span class="ex">the Jordan Lee who works for Northline</span>.</p>
  <p>Test the discourse, not just the punctuation. Ask: Can the listener recover the referent before the clause? Does the clause select a subset? If I remove the clause, does the intended person or thing remain clear? What separate claim disappears? Then punctuate the meaning you intend.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You used the stated family, namesake, group, and document contexts to choose defining or non-defining meaning instead of relying on noun type alone.">
