  <h2>Keep the relative role visible</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Inside a non-defining clause, the relative expression still fills a grammatical role. Use <span class="ex">who</span> for a person as subject; <span class="ex">who</span> or more formal <span class="ex">whom</span> for a person as object; <span class="ex">which</span> for a thing or proposition; <span class="ex">whose + noun</span> for association or possession; <span class="ex">where</span> for a place relationship; and <span class="ex">when</span> for a time relationship.</p>
  <p>Standard non-defining clauses do not use <span class="ex">that</span>, and the relative expression cannot be omitted even when it is an object: <span class="ex">The report, which I read yesterday, was clear</span>, not <span class="ex">The report, I read yesterday, was clear</span>. Do not repeat the represented noun with <span class="ex">he, she, it, there</span>, or another object inside the clause.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Whose</span> is not limited to people. It can connect organizations and things to an associated noun: <span class="ex">Northline, whose bid includes temporary ramps, starts in August</span>; <span class="ex">The policy, whose costs remain uncertain, is under review</span>. Agreement follows the noun after <span class="ex">whose</span>: <span class="ex">whose assistant works</span>, but <span class="ex">whose assistants work</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept subject who, object whom, object which, whose plus an associated noun, where, when, thing whose, and plural agreement inside complete non-defining clauses.">
