  <h2>Use prepositions and quantity patterns deliberately</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Conversation often leaves a preposition at the end: <span class="ex">Dana Ruiz, who I spoke with yesterday, will attend</span>. More formal prose can place the preposition before <span class="ex">whom</span> or <span class="ex">which</span>: <span class="ex">Dana Ruiz, with whom I spoke yesterday, will attend</span>; <span class="ex">The policy, under which staff may work remotely, expires in June</span>. Do not combine both positions.</p>
  <p>To comment on members of an identified set, use a quantity expression plus <span class="ex">of whom</span> for people or <span class="ex">of which</span> for things: <span class="ex">five advisers, all of whom...</span>; <span class="ex">ten changes, four of which...</span>. The quantity phrase supplies exact scope. The relative word cannot be replaced with <span class="ex">that</span>.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished conversational stranding, formal fronting, people and thing quantities, a zero quantity, a policy relationship, and a grant application relationship.">
