  <h2>Punctuate the meaning, not the length</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>When a non-defining clause interrupts a sentence, mark both boundaries: <span class="ex">The library, which opened in 1986, is being renovated.</span> When the clause comes at the end, one opening comma is enough: <span class="ex">The renovation begins in August, which is the quietest month.</span> Do not place a comma after the relative word, and do not add commas merely because a defining clause is long.</p>
  <p>Removing the inserted clause should leave a structurally complete main sentence, but it also removes a separate assertion. From <span class="ex">The library, which opened in 1986, is being renovated</span>, the main frame is <span class="ex">The library is being renovated</span>. The opening date disappears; the library’s identity remains.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used paired commas around an interruption, one comma before an ending comment, no comma for an identifying subgroup, no comma after the relative word, a complete main frame, and punctuation that preserves the intended set.">
