  <h2>Sound natural: signal a side comment without reading commas aloud</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Written commas do not require two fixed silent pauses. In U.S. speech, a non-defining clause often forms its own thought group, with a small boundary, a pitch reset, or a lighter parenthetical contour before the speaker returns to the main sentence: <span class="ex">Jordan Lee | who presented the PLAN | answered QUESTIONS.</span> The exact pause length varies with speed, emphasis, and clause length.</p>
  <p>A defining clause is more tightly integrated with the noun: <span class="ex">The consultants who speak SPANISH will lead the bilingual SESSIONS.</span> A non-defining reading can separate the already selected group: <span class="ex">The selected consultants | who ALL speak Spanish | will lead the SESSIONS.</span> In formal preposition-fronting, keep the phrase together: <span class="ex">with WHOM I spoke</span>. In conversation, <span class="ex">who I SPOKE with</span> is natural.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read defining integration, parenthetical thought groups, whole-clause which, formal preposition-fronting, and conversational stranding listening check" data-success-message="Correct. You heard an integrated subgroup, a parenthetical whole group, a whole-clause consequence, formal fronting, and conversational preposition stranding.">
